#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106562} :activity/id #uuid "a038488b-d4de-5f2b-9c61-0a4bc91a3757" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050394 :topic/id #uuid "455f5bc8-b430-5c8e-bb46-a11907e59c78" :topic/math-academy-id 1295} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106562 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107166 :tutorial/content "The **vector projection** of a vector $\\mathbf{a}$ onto another vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is the portion that the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ extends along $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nFor example, consider two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ shown below. Suppose we know that $|\\mathbf{a}|=2,$ $|\\mathbf{b}|=3,$ and the angle between the vectors is $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.$\n\n![](images/a2/a28278cf12885ab7f845407ac2fa7f4ffa00dbe30849a3f8ff41f02e134ed3a0.png)\n\nThe starting point of the vector projection is $O,$ and the ending point will be the point $P$ that lies on the line containing $\\mathbf{b}$ with $AP \\perp \\mathbf{b},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/f0/f0b267fb009e79eb16f86be42edc2c835808b28aa3eaf1c7e857300224ee01ec.png)\n\nThe vector $\\overrightarrow{OP}$ is called the **vector projection** of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}.$ It is denoted as $\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a},$ and it is computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a & =OP\\cdot u \\\\  & ={\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a\\cdot u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a} = |\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| \\cos\\theta$ is the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{u}$ is a unit vector in the direction of $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the scalar projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{b}}{|\\,\\mathbf{b}\\,|} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{b}}{3} = \\dfrac{1}{3} \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a & ={\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a\\cdot u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}b \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5962a19e-bb12-54d8-b53a-24578711fa53" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9051 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "26031206-512a-5b41-90d5-299987889655" :step/math-academy-id 121755 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106650}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106563 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105701 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105478 :question/id #uuid "3278333b-a29e-54c5-bc94-b5525bc6f751" :question/math-academy-id "e-14617"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c9317eb-d1ce-5f42-b69b-a37e643b865b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049653}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292817} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441580} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480688}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Vector Projection Using Angle and Magnitude"} :step/id #uuid "e6fd410f-37e5-5a4e-a29d-c160c7d2a397" :step/math-academy-id 121756 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106564}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106564 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107167 :tutorial/content "We have been using the following formula for the vector projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a} =\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{u}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can write an alternative formula using the formula for the scalar projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the original formula for the vector projection, and also substituting $\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{b}}{| \\, \\mathbf{b} \\, |},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|}\\cdot \\frac{b}{|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,{|}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, another general formula for the vector projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e4098700-6d5c-5618-90a8-8dea2d4dbed0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9052 :tutorial/title "A General Formula"} :step/id #uuid "a78e2420-8af1-50c7-9a19-b4ceac9bcde4" :step/math-academy-id 121759 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106565}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106565 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105702 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105479 :question/id #uuid "58b2269d-f998-5ba5-9df8-54ea422c43aa" :question/math-academy-id "e-14618"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0670172-77cb-5e88-b78d-e5136c06095f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106039} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287937} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292819} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292821} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297481} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299633} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299634} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299635} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387671} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441583} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480692}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Vector Projection Using Components"} :step/id #uuid "af715941-1577-597e-b254-6bc4aa300b8f" :step/math-academy-id 121760 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106567}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106566 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105703 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105480 :question/id #uuid "663f3f5e-56cf-5517-ba1e-d1a355767e0b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14619"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84c96375-27d5-5ada-a952-34e09b3e92bf" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299498} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441581} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480639} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516794}] :knowledge-point/title "Perpendicular Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "146819e4-ecad-5e47-8884-a05b59624d07" :step/math-academy-id 121763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106567 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107168 :tutorial/content "Notice that if two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are perpendicular, then by projecting $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ (or $\\mathbf{b}$ onto $\\mathbf{a}$), we obtain the zero vector.\n\nFor example, the projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto a perpendicular vector $\\mathbf{b}$ looks as follows:\n\n![](images/91/9163eda36b752f1e36195b8b5a1d40bf545ec21a3988f5480d6ddcbb926c00ea.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following fact:\n\n*If $\\mathbf{a}$ is a nonzero vector, its projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{b}$ equals the zero vector if and only if the vectors are perpendicular.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "97117437-4034-5cb8-84a6-675d75d4f874" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9059 :tutorial/title "Projecting Perpendicular Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "ec38d18e-577a-5ebc-8349-60991ef75b80" :step/math-academy-id 121766 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106566}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106650 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107181 :tutorial/content "When finding the projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b},$ we sometimes find that the resulting projection vector points in the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{b}.$ This happens when the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ extends in the direction opposite to that of $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n![](images/0e/0e973e6f6484a9aad863ebfe89fc1ea6356d6a50844931306c6aa2b63be18a8a.png)\n\nConsider two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b},$ where it is known that \n\n$$\n|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,|=2,\\qquad |\\,\\mathbf{b}\\,|=3,\\qquad \\theta = 135^\\circ.\n$$\n\nLet's calculate $\\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a}.$ \n\nFirst, notice that the angle between $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf b$ is obtuse. This means we expect the projection vector to point in the opposite direction to that of $\\mathbf b.$\n\nNext, we compute the scalar projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({135}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the scalar projection is negative. \n\nThen, we find the unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{b}}{|\\,\\mathbf{b}\\,|} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{b}}{3} = \\dfrac{1}{3} \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a & ={\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a\\cdot u \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis vector points in the direction opposite to $\\mathbf{b},$ as expected." :tutorial/id #uuid "2df5fde5-316c-5962-8f0d-56362287c447" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9677 :tutorial/title "The Direction of the Projection Vector"} :step/id #uuid "fdc39c18-bf51-5a32-a828-404d1e341f7a" :step/math-academy-id 187844 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106563}}] :activity/title "Calculating a Vector Projection" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105967}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487447} :activity/id #uuid "3f9712cf-1f13-53f6-99ad-54dc733817ad" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050394 :topic/id #uuid "455f5bc8-b430-5c8e-bb46-a11907e59c78" :topic/math-academy-id 1295} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487417 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299663 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa759f3a-59cf-57f1-9eb4-fe076883ee65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298620}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298620} :answer-field/id #uuid "415cdd53-3c35-5e12-a9ad-bdb260275079" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2940b787-7de0-58e6-a5d9-3477f08d2d69" :question/math-academy-id "q-317944" :question/problem "Let $a=t\\,i-4j+k$ and $b=4i+j.$ Given that the projection of $a$ onto $b$ is the zero vector, find the value of $t.$\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $a$ is a nonzero vector, its projection onto another nonzero vector $b$ equals the zero vector only when the vectors are perpendicular.\n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\na⊥b\\,⟹\\,a\\cdot b=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =t\\cdot 4+(-4)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =4t-4+0 \\\\  & =4t-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ 4t-4 & =0 \\\\ 4t & =4 \\\\ t & =1 \\\\ t & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "333ded1a-53b9-5952-8d65-b4f24b2126f6"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487424 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290296 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "085237b3-7339-507a-81da-41e8838a4e5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288096} {:answer/id #uuid "2547952c-6d6d-51dd-84d9-5d6dddcecc24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288278} {:answer/id #uuid "348d85f2-eb5b-5146-8401-4763298c0ead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288381} {:answer/id #uuid "81de0d68-8b33-5042-b528-9c369770b979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288908} {:answer/id #uuid "983ed9cb-19d2-5826-8970-1323081a65d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289052}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289052} :answer-field/id #uuid "5656f1d3-8dfe-58cd-9391-206ef0a86ac4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289911}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cbcf41af-6f81-5a46-9b85-441a34ac9cb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-212459" :question/problem "Let $a=⟨2,-1,-2⟩$ and $b=⟨x,-4,0⟩.$ Given that the projection of $a$ onto $b$ is the zero vector, find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $a$ is a nonzero vector, its projection onto another nonzero vector $b$ equals the zero vector only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\na⊥b\\,⟹\\,a\\cdot b=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =2\\cdot x+(-1)\\cdot (-4)+(-2)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ 2x+4 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =-4 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4f577824-992f-5ecb-84d6-749721d2f2ab" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487441}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487436 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287938 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "333bcad1-e70c-5829-9062-c43fba448878" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨6,-6,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285255} {:answer/id #uuid "567c3699-d085-5a43-943c-ac24a5cb251c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨3,-3,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285583} {:answer/id #uuid "a2209f52-c5f3-545b-acd4-9063189003cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286273} {:answer/id #uuid "c25020f3-be37-5e8b-a50f-1b4e8f6258eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{3}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286566} {:answer/id #uuid "c9bb4760-d5c8-5ecf-a28e-d323c63d492e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨2,-2,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286628}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286628} :answer-field/id #uuid "04959729-9f39-58a0-94d2-f287c161fe10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287151}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "60b66f90-3842-5d62-9e02-b5d4a4919337" :question/math-academy-id "q-19214" :question/problem "Let $p=⟨1,-1,1⟩$ and $q=⟨8,4,2⟩.$ Find the vector projection of $q$ onto $p.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the vector projection, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{p}\\,q & =\\frac{p\\cdot q}{p\\cdot p}p \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{{p}_{x}^{2}+{p}_{y}^{2}+{p}_{z}^{2}}p \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 8+(-1)\\cdot 4+1\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}p \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3}⟨1,-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "acc822ee-4c60-569f-b9f3-f99814a03662" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487424}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487441 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299659 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1e74cda-962f-530c-9dc5-da6fa3027618" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298576}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298576} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ebbdb00-7250-5b52-a3d7-5e3a06732032" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298855} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c9bd58c-c3aa-5aac-8af2-0f2dd4d21cd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298091}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298091} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ca49e40-42ef-549f-8cba-a8415f643eee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298892} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26a218fd-1c69-51ca-837a-ec2ac174e881" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297712}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297712} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a211abc-329d-5e8e-a21a-d845d97c0b27" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299145}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "47bb7ff9-8595-5c3c-8469-6716c5ae622e" :question/math-academy-id "q-317924" :question/problem "Let $u=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find the vector projection of $u$ onto $v.$\n\n${\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,u=⟨$\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}\n$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the vector projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,u & =\\frac{u\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}_{x}{v}_{x}+{u}_{y}{v}_{y}+{u}_{z}{v}_{z}}{{v}_{x}^{2}+{v}_{y}^{2}+{v}_{z}^{2}}v \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 1+(1)\\cdot 0}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}v \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{5}v \\\\  & =⟨\\,0,0,0\\,⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d23f251b-88e1-5993-bf53-204aefc296ce" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487417}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487447 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287935 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00b763df-9c1f-5e4b-9796-546c1233f800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{57}{35},\\frac{19}{14},-\\frac{57}{70}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284797} {:answer/id #uuid "2d331e62-52ea-521a-a944-b3adad2ca0d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{57}{35},\\frac{19}{14},-\\frac{57}{35}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285189} {:answer/id #uuid "88e9eee0-e73f-5083-b2a8-8bc4aeb6be2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{57}{35},\\frac{19}{35},-\\frac{57}{35}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286042} {:answer/id #uuid "ee59770c-2622-5f86-9233-135b35d7ab48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{57}{70},\\frac{19}{14},-\\frac{57}{70}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186